---
import Base from "../../../layouts/Base.astro";
import SubNav from "../../../components/SubNav.astro";
import Section from "../../../components/Section.astro";
import Card from "../../../components/Card.astro";

const runMap = `{
  "generator": "stacked-rooms@1",   // bump when output changes
  "seed": 18446744073709551615,     // u64, from the server only
  "params": {
    "depth": 3,                     // stacked floors
    "level_band": [4, 7],           // party level the map is aimed at
    "biome": "crypt",
    "party_size": 4
  },
  "spawn": [0.0, 0.0, 0.0],
  "exit":  [12.0, 6.5, -3.0]        // the way home, always a portal
}`;

const dungeonLayout = `{
  "layout_version": 3,
  "extent": [48, 24, 48],           // metres, one plot per player
  "floors": 2,
  "stations": [
    { "kind": "forge",   "at": [6.0, 0.0, 2.0] },
    { "kind": "kitchen", "at": [9.0, 0.0, 2.0] },
    { "kind": "lab",     "at": [12.0, 0.0, 2.0] }
  ],
  "portal_anchor": [24.0, 0.0, 40.0],
  "door": { "policy": "public", "listed": true }
}`;
---

<Base title="Dungeon data & schema — Party Dungeon" current="/mechanics/dungeon/">
  <SubNav current="/mechanics/dungeon/" />

  <header>
    <div class="kicker">
      <a href="/mechanics/">Mechanics</a> /
      <a href="/mechanics/dungeon/">Your dungeon</a> / Data &amp; schema
    </div>
    <h1>Two maps, and only one of them is stored.</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      A run map is a seed. Your dungeon is a document. They look like the same
      kind of thing and behave nothing alike &mdash; mixing them up is the
      expensive mistake in this schema.
    </p>
  </header>

  <Section title="Two kinds of map data">
    <div class="split">
      <Card status="decided" title="Run maps are generated" tag="Yours">
        <p>
          Maps you portal into are <strong>procedurally generated</strong>.
          Nobody authored them, and no two runs need the same one.
        </p>
      </Card>
      <Card status="guess" title="So a run map is stored as a seed">
        <p>
          The server keeps <code>seed</code>, <code>generator_version</code>,
          and the parameters it was called with &mdash; then regenerates the
          geometry on demand. The map itself is never a row.
        </p>
      </Card>
      <Card status="decided" title="Your dungeon is authored" tag="Yours">
        <p>
          The base is a <strong>place you build in</strong>. A seed cannot
          reproduce edits nobody has made yet.
        </p>
      </Card>
      <Card status="guess" title="So your dungeon is a document">
        <p>
          Its layout is stored, versioned, and loaded back verbatim. This is
          the only dungeon geometry that touches the database.
        </p>
      </Card>
    </div>
  </Section>

  <Section title="What a run map is made of">
    <p class="lede" style="font-size:16px; margin-bottom:18px">
      Deterministic input, one output. Same version and same seed must produce
      the same map on every machine, or replays and anti-cheat both fall apart.
    </p>
    <pre><code>{runMap}</code></pre>
    <div class="grid" style="margin-top:16px">
      <Card status="guess" title="Version is part of the identity">
        <p>
          Changing the generator changes every map. Old runs keep the version
          they were created with, so <code>generator_version</code> is a
          column, not a constant.
        </p>
      </Card>
      <Card status="guess" title="The server hands out the seed">
        <p>
          A client that picks its own seed picks its own loot. The seed is
          generated server-side and returned with the run record.
        </p>
      </Card>
    </div>
  </Section>

  <Section title="What your dungeon is made of">
    <pre><code>{dungeonLayout}</code></pre>
    <div class="grid" style="margin-top:16px">
      <Card status="guess" title="One document, not twenty tables">
        <p>
          Placed objects are a list inside one <code>jsonb</code> column.
          Normalising every chair into a row buys nothing while the editor is
          still being designed.
        </p>
      </Card>
      <Card status="guess" title="Versioned on every save">
        <p>
          <code>layout_version</code> increments per write. Visitors load a
          snapshot; if the owner edits mid-visit, the visit keeps what it
          entered.
        </p>
      </Card>
    </div>
  </Section>

  <Section title="Who owns the truth">
    <div class="grid">
      <Card status="guess" title="The server simulates, the client renders">
        <p>
          Loot is teleported home, dungeons are opened to strangers, items are
          sold. Every one of those is a trust boundary, so the server decides
          and the database is the record.
        </p>
      </Card>
      <Card status="guess" title="Runs are records, not state">
        <p>
          A run is a row that opens and closes. Its live state lives in memory
          on the server; only the outcome is written.
        </p>
      </Card>
    </div>
  </Section>

  <Section title="Database shape">
    <Card>
      <ul class="plain">
        <li><b>accounts</b> &mdash; one per player. Owns characters, dungeons, items.</li>
        <li><b>characters</b> &mdash; class, level, stats. The thing a run puts at risk.</li>
        <li><b>parties</b> &mdash; the assembled roster. Shape still open, see below.</li>
        <li><b>dungeons</b> &mdash; owner, layout <code>jsonb</code>, <code>layout_version</code>, door policy.</li>
        <li><b>dungeon_visits</b> &mdash; who walked in, when, what they bought. Traffic is the point of opening the doors.</li>
        <li><b>items</b> &mdash; instances: definition, level, runes, stats, crafted_by, location.</li>
        <li><b>item_definitions</b>, <b>runes</b> &mdash; the crafting catalogue, data not code.</li>
        <li><b>runs</b> &mdash; seed, generator version, params, party, outcome.</li>
        <li><b>listings</b> &mdash; market rows: item, seller, price, state.</li>
      </ul>
    </Card>
    <div class="grid" style="margin-top:16px">
      <Card status="guess" title="Postgres, and jsonb where it will churn">
        <p>
          The game logic is Rust with <code>sqlx</code>. Layouts, stats, and
          rune effects are <code>jsonb</code> until their shape stops moving;
          anything queried or joined on gets a real column.
        </p>
      </Card>
      <Card status="guess" title="Items are immutable; ownership moves">
        <p>
          Runes and level are fixed at craft time. Selling or stashing changes
          <code>owner</code> and <code>location</code> only &mdash; never the
          item's identity. Otherwise a "used" sword means something different
          every patch.
        </p>
      </Card>
      <Card status="guess" title="Dungeons soft-delete">
        <p>
          Visitors have been there, items point at it, and runs reference it.
          Deleting the plot leaves the record and marks it closed.
        </p>
      </Card>
      <Card status="guess" title="No sharding yet">
        <p>
          One database. A player owns one plot and a handful of items; the
          write rate is nothing until dungeons get busy. Revisit when
          <code>dungeon_visits</code> is the biggest table.
        </p>
      </Card>
    </div>
  </Section>

  <Section title="Open questions">
    <Card>
      <ul class="plain">
        <li>
          <b>Is a party one player's roster, or four players?</b> This decides
          whether <code>parties</code> points at an account or at a set of
          accounts. Everything downstream changes with it.
        </li>
        <li>
          <b>Do items need provenance?</b> "Crafted by the smith who sold it to
          you" is a nice story and a join table. Is it a feature?
        </li>
        <li>
          <b>Does the market hold escrow?</b> Listing an item has to take it
          out of circulation, or two players sell the same sword.
        </li>
        <li>
          <b>What happens to a run when the server dies?</b> Roll back the run,
          keep the loot, or keep nothing? This is the player-facing one.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </Card>
  </Section>
</Base>
